---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/Callout.astro';
import Faq from '../../components/Faq.astro';
import AutoRelated from '../../components/AutoRelated.astro';
import authors from '../../data/authors.json';
import municipalData from '../../data/maine-municipal-authorization.json';
import optInData from '../../data/maine-opt-in-towns.json';

const article = {
  author: "Calvin Waters",
  authorId: "calvin-waters",
  authorTitle: "Licensing & Compliance Analyst",
  publishDate: "2026-07-21",
  modifiedDate: "2026-08-11",
  section: "Policy & Legislation"
};
const topics = ["policy", "legislation", "municipal", "operator-guide"];

// Compute stats from our proprietary dataset
const municipalities = municipalData.municipalities;
const totalMunicipalities = municipalities.length;
const authorized = municipalities.filter(m => m.status === 'retail_authorized');
const optedOut = municipalities.filter(m => m.status === 'explicit_opt_out');
const unknown = municipalities.filter(m => m.status === 'no_recorded_retail_authorization');
const authorizedCount = authorized.length;
const optedOutCount = optedOut.length;
const unknownCount = unknown.length;
const linkedActiveStoreCount = optInData.towns.reduce((sum, town) => sum + town.dispensaries_operating, 0);
const authorizedPct = Math.round((authorizedCount / totalMunicipalities) * 100);

// Group authorized towns by county
const byCounty: Record<string, string[]> = {};
for (const m of authorized) {
  if (!byCounty[m.county]) byCounty[m.county] = [];
  byCounty[m.county].push(m.name);
}
const countyEntries = Object.entries(byCounty).sort((a, b) => b[1].length - a[1].length);

// Top 10 counties for the table
const topCounties = countyEntries.slice(0, 10);

const faqItems = [
  { question: "How many towns in Maine allow dispensaries?", answer: `As of the OCP opt-in data refreshed ${municipalData.meta.ocp_model_refreshed_at.split('T')[0]}, ${authorizedCount} of Maine's ${totalMunicipalities} incorporated municipalities (${authorizedPct}%) are recorded as authorized for adult-use retail. The canonical model has ${optedOutCount} evidence-backed explicit opt-outs and ${unknownCount} municipalities with no recorded retail authorization; an OCP N activity flag alone is not treated as proof of an opt-out.` },
  { question: "Does my town allow a dispensary?", answer: "Check our Municipal Authorization Tracker, then confirm the current local record with the municipal clerk. If the tracker shows 'no recorded retail authorization,' the OCP model does not establish the municipality's present local position; do not treat that status as authorization to open." },
  { question: "Can a town change its dispensary rules after authorizing retail?", answer: "Municipal authorization and local ordinances can change, but the effect on existing licensees depends on current state law and the municipality's ordinance. Do not assume grandfathering or a transition period; verify the current rule with the municipal clerk and OCP." },
  { question: "What does 'no recorded authorization' mean?", answer: "It means only that the OCP model does not record adult-use retail authorization for that municipality. It does not establish whether the municipality took no action, rejected a proposal, adopted a prohibition, or acted in some other way. Verify the current local record with the municipal clerk." },
  { question: "Which county has the most dispensary-friendly towns?", answer: `York County leads with ${byCounty['York']?.length || 0} authorized municipalities, followed by Kennebec (${byCounty['Kennebec']?.length || 0}), Penobscot (${byCounty['Penobscot']?.length || 0}), and Oxford (${byCounty['Oxford']?.length || 0}). Southern and central Maine counties are generally more permissive than rural northern counties.` },
  { question: "Do I need both town approval AND a state license?", answer: "Yes. Maine's adult-use licensing process requires written municipal authorization from the host municipality and the applicable state OCP license. Confirm the current municipal and state requirements before signing a lease or beginning operations." },
  { question: "How do I ask a town to authorize adult-use retail?", answer: "Start with the municipal clerk. Local procedures are ordinance-specific and may involve a council, select board, planning board, public hearing, town meeting, referendum, or another process. Ask for the current ordinance, application requirements, meeting schedule, and decision timeline rather than relying on a statewide estimate." },
  { question: "Are there towns with moratoriums on dispensaries?", answer: "Some Maine towns have enacted temporary moratoriums while they develop cannabis regulations. A moratorium is not a permanent ban — it pauses new applications until the town adopts rules. Check with your town clerk for current moratorium status." }
];
---

<Layout
  title="Which Towns Allow Dispensaries in Maine? 78 of 485 (2026 Data)"
  description="78 of Maine's 485 municipalities have authorized retail cannabis sales. Full county-by-county breakdown from OCP opt-in data, plus how to check your town and get municipal approval."
  heroImage="/images/heroes/ocp-license-map.jpg"
  article={article}
  topics={topics}
>
<style>
  article { max-width: 42rem; margin: 0 auto; }
  .article-header { margin-bottom: 2rem; padding-bottom: 1.5rem; border-bottom: 2px solid var(--color-accent); }
  .article-header h1 { font-size: 2.25rem; line-height: 1.2; color: var(--color-primary); margin-bottom: 1rem; }
  .subtitle { font-size: 1.2rem; color: var(--color-text-light); font-style: italic; }
  .answer-capsule { font-size: 1.15rem; line-height: 1.7; background: color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-soft-green) 8%, transparent); border-left: 4px solid var(--color-primary); padding: 1.25rem 1.5rem; border-radius: 0 0.5rem 0.5rem 0; margin: 1.5rem 0; }
  section { margin-bottom: 2.5rem; }
  h2 { font-size: 1.6rem; color: var(--color-primary); margin-top: 2rem; margin-bottom: 1rem; padding-bottom: 0.4rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-accent); }
  h3 { font-size: 1.2rem; color: var(--color-primary); margin-top: 1.5rem; margin-bottom: 0.75rem; }
  p { line-height: 1.7; margin-bottom: 1rem; }
  ul, ol { padding-left: 1.5rem; margin-bottom: 1rem; }
  li { line-height: 1.7; margin-bottom: 0.5rem; }
  table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; margin: 1.5rem 0; font-size: 0.95rem; }
  th, td { padding: 0.6rem 0.75rem; text-align: left; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border); }
  th { background: var(--color-background); font-weight: 700; }
  .stat-block { background-color: color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-soft-green) 6%, transparent); border: 1px solid var(--color-accent); border-radius: 0.5rem; padding: 1.5rem 2rem; margin: 2rem 0; }
  .stat-block h3 { margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 1rem; color: var(--color-primary); }
  .stat-block ul { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 0; }
  .stat-block li { padding: 0.5rem 0; border-bottom: 1px solid color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-soft-green) 15%, transparent); }
  .stat-block li:last-child { border-bottom: none; }
  .byline { font-size: 0.95rem; color: var(--color-text-light); margin-top: 1.5rem; padding-top: 1rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border); }
  .byline p { margin: 0.25rem 0; }
  .disclaimer { background: var(--color-disclaimer-bg); border: 1px solid var(--color-disclaimer-border); padding: 1rem; border-radius: 0.5rem; font-size: 0.875rem; color: var(--color-disclaimer-text); margin-top: 2rem; }
  .county-grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(280px, 1fr)); gap: 1rem; margin: 1.5rem 0; }
  .county-card { background: var(--color-background); border: 1px solid var(--color-border); border-radius: 0.5rem; padding: 1rem; }
  .county-card h4 { margin: 0 0 0.5rem; color: var(--color-primary); font-size: 1rem; }
  .county-card ul { margin: 0; padding-left: 1.25rem; font-size: 0.9rem; }
  .county-card li { margin-bottom: 0.25rem; line-height: 1.4; }
</style>

<article>
  <div class="article-header">
    <h1>Which Towns Allow Dispensaries in Maine? {authorizedCount} of {totalMunicipalities} ({authorizedPct}%)</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">The complete county-by-county breakdown of Maine's cannabis opt-in system — from OCP open data, refreshed {municipalData.meta.ocp_model_refreshed_at.split('T')[0]}</p>
    <div class="byline">
      <p>By <a href="/about/authors#calvin-waters"><strong>Calvin Waters</strong></a>, Licensing & Compliance Analyst. <strong>Data reviewed by the Maine Cannabis editorial team.</strong></p>
      <p>Published {article.publishDate} · Updated {article.modifiedDate}</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="answer-capsule">
    <strong>{authorizedCount} of Maine's {totalMunicipalities} incorporated municipalities ({authorizedPct}%) are recorded as authorized for adult-use retail cannabis sales</strong> as of the OCP opt-in data refreshed {municipalData.meta.ocp_model_refreshed_at.split('T')[0]}. The canonical model has {optedOutCount} evidence-backed explicit opt-outs and {unknownCount} municipalities with no recorded retail authorization. An OCP <code>N</code> activity flag alone is not treated as proof of a local rejection vote.
  </p>

  <section>
    <h2>The Numbers at a Glance</h2>
    <div class="stat-block">
      <h3>Maine Municipal Cannabis Authorization ({municipalData.meta.ocp_model_refreshed_at.split('T')[0]})</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Total municipalities tracked:</strong> {totalMunicipalities} (23 cities, 431 towns, 31 plantations)</li>
        <li><strong>Retail authorized:</strong> {authorizedCount} ({authorizedPct}%)</li>
        <li><strong>Evidence-backed explicit opt-outs in canonical model:</strong> {optedOutCount} ({Math.round((optedOutCount / totalMunicipalities) * 100)}%)</li>
        <li><strong>No recorded retail authorization:</strong> {unknownCount} ({Math.round((unknownCount / totalMunicipalities) * 100)}%)</li>
        <li><strong>Active adult-use Store licenses linked to authorized municipalities:</strong> {linkedActiveStoreCount} (OCP roster retrieved 2026-08-11)</li>
        <li><strong>Data source:</strong> <a href="https://www.maine.gov/dafs/ocp/open-data/adult-use/opt-in-communities" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">OCP Opt-In Communities Open Data</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>How Maine's Opt-In System Works</h2>
    <p>Maine's adult-use cannabis framework (Title 28-B, Chapter 1) gives municipalities the power to decide whether cannabis businesses can operate within their borders. This is an <strong>opt-in system</strong>: a town must affirmatively vote to allow retail cannabis sales before any dispensary can open there.</p>
    <p>The practical implication: municipal authorization is required before OCP can issue an active establishment license. OCP's published sequence is conditional licensure, then local authorization, then active licensure. A "no recorded retail authorization" status in this snapshot is not proof of the municipality's current local position; confirm it with the municipal clerk.</p>
    <Callout title="No Recorded Authorization ≠ Banned" type="info">
      {unknownCount} municipalities ({Math.round((unknownCount / totalMunicipalities) * 100)}% of Maine) show "no recorded retail authorization." This status means only that the OCP model does not record authorization; it does not establish whether the municipality took no action, rejected a proposal, adopted a prohibition, or acted in some other way. Confirm the current local record with the municipal clerk.
    </Callout>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Top Counties by Authorized Municipalities</h2>
    <p>Southern and central Maine counties are the most dispensary-friendly. Here are the top 10:</p>
    <table aria-label="Top Maine counties by number of retail-authorized municipalities">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>County</th><th>Authorized Towns</th><th>Notable Municipalities</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {topCounties.map(([county, towns]) => (
          <tr>
            <td><strong>{county}</strong></td>
            <td>{towns.length}</td>
            <td>{towns.slice(0, 4).join(', ')}{towns.length > 4 ? ` +${towns.length - 4} more` : ''}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>All {authorizedCount} Retail-Authorized Municipalities by County</h2>
    <p>The complete list, grouped by county. Use our <a href="/guides/maine-cannabis-opt-in-tracker">interactive Municipal Authorization Tracker</a> to search for a specific town.</p>
    <div class="county-grid">
      {countyEntries.map(([county, towns]) => (
        <div class="county-card">
          <h4>{county} County ({towns.length})</h4>
          <ul>
            {towns.sort().map(town => <li>{town}</li>)}
          </ul>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>How to Check If Your Town Allows Dispensaries</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Search our tracker:</strong> The <a href="/guides/maine-cannabis-opt-in-tracker">Municipal Authorization Tracker</a> lists all {totalMunicipalities} municipalities with their current retail authorization status.</li>
      <li><strong>Verify with the town clerk:</strong> OCP data can lag behind local votes. Call or email your town clerk to confirm current status.</li>
      <li><strong>Check for pending votes:</strong> Some towns have cannabis ordinances on upcoming town meeting agendas. A "no recorded authorization" town may vote within weeks.</li>
      <li><strong>Confirm zoning and school setbacks:</strong> Even in an authorized municipality, the address must satisfy local zoning and state requirements. Maine's default school setback is 1,000 feet; a municipality may adopt a smaller setback, but not less than 500 feet. Verify the current ordinance and measurement method for the specific site.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>How to Ask a Municipality About Authorization</h2>
    <p>If the current local record does not authorize adult-use retail, start with the municipal clerk. Maine municipalities use ordinance-specific procedures, so there is no single statewide petition path or reliable statewide timeline.</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Request the governing documents:</strong> Ask for the current cannabis ordinance, zoning map, application requirements, fee schedule, and any moratorium.</li>
      <li><strong>Identify the decision-maker:</strong> Depending on the municipality, authority may involve a council, select board, planning board, town meeting, referendum, or another local process.</li>
      <li><strong>Check the official schedule:</strong> Ask which meetings, notices, hearings, or votes apply and when a complete request would be considered.</li>
      <li><strong>Confirm state sequencing:</strong> OCP's published process places conditional licensure before municipal authorization and active licensure after it.</li>
      <li><strong>Consider legal counsel:</strong> A Maine land-use attorney can interpret the specific ordinance and advise on the proposed site.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>For the full step-by-step process, see our <a href="/guides/maine-cannabis-municipal-opt-in-guide">Municipal Opt-In Guide</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <Faq faqs={faqItems} />
  </section>

  <section class="disclaimer">
    <p><em>This data is sourced from the Maine Office of Cannabis Policy's open-data opt-in communities list, refreshed {municipalData.meta.ocp_model_refreshed_at.split('T')[0]}. Municipal authorization status can change between data refreshes. Always verify current status with your town clerk and the OCP before making business decisions. This is not legal advice.</em></p>
  </section>

  <AutoRelated currentPath="/blog/which-towns-allow-dispensaries-in-maine" currentTopics={topics} section="Policy & Legislation" limit={6} />
</article>
</Layout>
